  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 AM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nThis workweek we will see a fairly decent break from the heat, but \nnot before another hot day today -- especially far southwest Kansas. \nLatest short term model guidance is highly suggestive of afternoon \ntemperatures in the 104 to 106F range from Liberal to Elkhart north \nto around Syracuse. It will be a dry heat, though, with dewpoints \ngenerally in the upper 40s to lower 50s. This will result in Heat \nIndex values several degrees lower than the actual air temperature. \nDespite this, we have collaborated with NWS Amarillo in the issuance \nof a Heat Advisory mainly due to the actual air temperature and heat \nstress potential to agriculture interests. A fairly strong south \nwind 15 to 25 mph should alleviate some of this heat stress, but \ndirect insolation will occur all day with large scale subsidence \nresulting in essentially no clouds. \n\nA rather strong shortwave trough will be moving east across the far \nnorthern CONUS starting Tuesday, and this will bring a polar front \nsouth into Kansas. This front will then stall out and become the \nfocus for multiple shower and thunderstorm chances through about \nThursday Night, at which point the frontal zone will dissolve as the \nsummer subtropical high builds back in. The latest NBM shows 40 to \n60% chance of 1/4\" or more QPF over a 48-hr period from Tuesday \nNight to Thursday Night. The frontal zone in place and outflow \nboundaries will result in a difficult temperature forecast, and this \nis seen in the NBM with 10-90th percentile ensemble spread of 10 to \n15 degrees for highs Tuesday and Wednesday with 10th percentile high \ntemperatures as low as the mid 70s across portions of west central \nKansas. \n\nThe pattern will revert back to favoring very hot temperatures as we \nhead into next weekend with 100 to 105F highs increasingly \nlikely...particularly August 9th and beyond.",
